  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000122568,
      "SummaryText": "PUBLIC NOTICE\nIn accordance with P.L. 106-40, on this date, June 12, 2025, River Country Co-op is publicly posting a summary of our Anhydrous Ammonia Risk Management Plan.  This facility meets the definition of a small business stationary source in section 507(c) of the Clean Air Act.\n\nRiver Country Co-op\nDorchester Agronomy Location\n181 Linden Street\nDorchester, WI 54425\n\nFor further information, contact Joe Theis, Agronomy Location Manager.\n\nRisk Management Plan - EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n\n1.\tThe Facility Policy\n\nThe owners, management, and employees of River Country Co-op are committed to the prevention of any accidental release of anhydrous ammonia.  If an accidental release should occur, the facility is prepared to work with the local fire department, or other authorities, to mitigate the release and minimize the impact of the release to people and the environment.\n\n2.\tFacility Information\n\n* The primary activity at the facility is the storage, and distribution of fertilizers for sale to farmers.\n* Anhydrous ammonia is received by a vendor,  and stored and distributed by River Country for direct application by the Farmers to apply on their fields. River Country fills both River Country and Farmers vessels. River Country will transport only the River Country Vessels to the customer and does not perform the spreading. Or the customer will transport their own vessel to their farm after filled by River County.\nRiver Country employees do not perform any spreading.\n* The maximum quantity stored would be 60,000 pounds in our 18,000-gallon storage tank.\n\n3. The worst-case release scenario and the alternate release scenario.\n\nA. The worst-case release scenario would be the release of the total contents of a storage tank released as a gas over 10 minutes. The maximum quantity released 60,000 pounds, which represents the volume of the largest storage tank at 85 percent capacity as limited by design standards. The distance to endpoint (point of dispersion to 200 ppm) is 3.3 miles"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000122568,
      "SummaryText": ".\n\nB. The alternative release scenario is a release from the break in a transfer hose. The distance to endpoint (point of dispersion to 200 ppm) is .5 miles.\n\n4. The accidental release prevention program.\n\nThe facility has implemented the provisions of \"Safety Requirements for the storage and Handling of Anhydrous Ammonia, K-61.1\", published by the American National Standards Institute, Inc., and the standards of the U.S. Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA), 29 CFR 1910.111, \"Storage and handling of anhydrous ammonia\".\n\n\n\n5. The Five-year Accident History\n\nThis facility has had two (2) accidents in the last five years.\n1. 8/2021 - Ruptured liquid valve line due to extreme weather conditions. No injuries reported.\n2. 6/2022 - A quick gas release due to extreme weather conditions exposed worker.  Injured worker was medically treated.\n\n6. The emergency response program\n\nThe facility has:\n\nA. A written emergency action plan, in accordance with OSHA standard, 29 CFR 1910.38.\nB. Provided state and local authorities the emergency planning and community right-to-know information as required under SARA Title III (EPCRA).\nC. A written emergency response program, in accordance with OSHA standard, 29 CFR 1910.120, including pre-emergency planning and employee training.\nD. A written Risk Management Plan with Clark County Emergency Planning Department that is reviewed annually and due by end of May.\n\n7. Planned changes to improve safety.\n\nSafety improvement is an on-going process at the facility. Periodic evaluations are performed to access the maintenance of safe conditions. There are no additional specific anhydrous ammonia safety recommendations for implementation at this time."
    }
